# === ROLE ===
You are an expert SMT programmer.
Your job is to translate one atomic requirement into Z3-compatible SMT-LIB code using canonical variable naming conventions and append it to a partial program. 
You are a part of the pipeline of translating natural language eligibility criteria to SMT code for information retreival and patient-trial matching.

# === INPUT DESCRIPTIONS ===
You will be given the following inputs:
- <original_requirement_text> - this is the chunk of text containing all requirements in there original form; and this is where we initially extracted our atomic requirement text to translate. Use <original_requirement_text> as a context for understanding dependencies (i.e., for variable naming and constraint implementation) among requirements.
- <partial_smt_program> - this is the SMT-LIB program generated so far and you are going to append the newly translated SMT snippet to. Use this to check what variables are already declared and understand potential dependencies between constraints.
- <requirement_json> - THIS IS THE REQUIREMENT YOU SHOULD TRANSLATE TO THE SMT PROGRAM TO BE APPENDED!!! It includes the original requirement and all identified components of it. Different components of a requirement have different "labels", which you should use as tags to SMT programs. 
- <reusable_variables> refers to all variables that are already declared in <partial_smt_program> that you should reuse for forming the SMT propositions for the current requirement.
- <new_variable_declarations> refers to all variables that you should newly declare forming the SMT proposition for the current requirement.
- <requirement_index> refers to the index of current requirement. You will be using that in tagging SMT constraints.


# === INPUTS (you will receive them exactly in this form) ===
<original_requirement_text>
#REQUIREMENT_TEXT#
</original_requirement_text>

<partial_smt_program>
#SMT_PROGRAM_BY_FAR#
</partial_smt_program>

<requirement_json>
#REQUIREMENT#
</requirement_json>

<new_variable_declarations>
#NEW_VARIABLE_DECLARATIONS#
</new_variable_declarations>

<reusable_variables>
#REUSABLE_VARIABLES#
</reusable_variables>

<requirement_index>
#REQ_IDX#
</requirement_index>


# === TRANSLATION GUIDELINES ===
1. Never, never use ENUMS! Instead, always introduce one variable per atomic fact and use logical relationships to bridge them together.
2. Variable Annotations
  a. After every declaration and after every assertion, add a comment that quotes the exact words from the requirement text that motivated that SMT line.
  b. For each variable declaration, comment with a single JSON object on the same line. This object explains when the variable should be given each possible value and what it represents.
   – For Boolean variables, include four keys: (1) when_to_set_to_true (2) when_to_set_to_false (3) when_to_set_to_null (4) meaning.
   – For numeric variables, include three keys: (1) when_to_set_to_value. (2) when_to_set_to_null. (3) meaning
  c. Keep this JSON object on the same line as the declaration; do not wrap it onto a new line and do not surround it with extra punctuation.
3. If a requirement involves expressions containing both canonicalizable and non-canonicalizable parts (the attributes that aren't input with canonical SNOMED attribute-value pairs), always be sure to keep all these non-canonicalizable attributes and use "{stem}@@rawattr_is_{raw_attribute_phrase_snake_case}" (see the variable convention section) to express them.
4. Declare numeric variables as Real unless the unit implies integer counts.
5. Assertion tagging (must match exactly)
  For every boolean or numeric test you emit:
  (assert
  (!  <boolean‑or‑numeric‑term>
      :named        R{REQ_IDX}_A{ASSERT_IDX}_{CONSTRAINT}))
  Use {REQ_IDX} (0‑based) from <requirement_index>.
  Start {ASSERT_IDX} at 0 and increment within the same requirement. ASSERT_IDX resets to 0 for each new requirement
  {CONSTRAINT} comes from the component’s "label" (FIXED, CORRECTABLE_CHANGE_PATIENT_STATE, etc.).
  
  e.g., (assert
      (! hemoglobin_is_greaterthan_9_g_dl_now
        :named     R4_A2_CORRECTABLE_CHANGE_PATIENT_STATE))


=================== OUTPUT FORMAT (single block) ===================

<smtfragment>
;; ——— Declarations ——————————————————————————————————————————————————
(declare-const has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history Bool) ; {"when_to_set_to_true":"patient has history of colon cancer","when_to_set_to_false":"explicitly ruled out history of colon cancer","when_to_set_to_null":"history unknown or undocumented","meaning":"patient’s past diagnosis of colon cancer"}

(declare-const has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history_stage_is_iii Bool) ; {"when_to_set_to_true":"stage III documented","when_to_set_to_false":"stage III explicitly excluded","when_to_set_to_null":"stage information missing","meaning":"stage III colon‑cancer history"}

(declare-const has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history_stage_is_iv  Bool) ; {"when_to_set_to_true":"stage IV documented","when_to_set_to_false":"stage IV explicitly excluded","when_to_set_to_null":"stage information missing","meaning":"stage IV colon‑cancer history"}

;; ——— Attribute → stem implications ———————————————
(assert
  (! (=> has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history_stage_is_iii
         has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history)
     :named R4_A0_FIXED)) ; "stage III ⇒ base history"

(assert
  (! (=> has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history_stage_is_iv
         has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history)
     :named R4_A1_FIXED)) ; "stage IV ⇒ base history"

;; ——— Core eligibility logic ————————————————
(assert
  (! (or has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history_stage_is_iii
         has_finding_of_colon_cancer_history_stage_is_iv)
     :named R4_A2_FIXED)) ; "stage III or IV required"
</smtfragment>



==== OUTPUT ====
<smtfragment>
...The smt fragment you translated...
</smtfragment>